ntion:\n    - The attention weights are spread uniformly across the encoder time steps. This indicates that the model has not yet learned to focus on specific regions of the input sequence that are most relevant for generating the output sequence.\n\n3. Diagonal Pattern (Absent):\n    - In well-trained models, attention weights often form a diagonal pattern, where each decoder time step focuses on a corresponding encoder time step. The absence of such a pattern here suggests that the model is still in the early stages of training and has not yet learned meaningful alignments.\n\n4. Low Attention Scores:\n    - The low attention scores (close to 0) indicate that the model is not confident in its predictions. This is expected after only 10 epochs of training, as the model is still learning to align the input and output sequences.\n\n## Expected Behavior After More Training\n1. Diagonal Pattern: The attention weights should form a diagonal pattern, indicating that each decoder time step focuses on the corresponding encoder time step.\n\n2. Localized Focus: The attention weights should become more localized, with higher scores concentrated around relevant parts of the input sequence.\n\n3. Higher Scores: The attention scores should increase, reflecting the model's confidence in its predictions.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"#dunno why ctc need dummy train_output  tbh","metadata":{}}]}